On 6/23/05 5:36 AM, Alexis wrote:

> I do quite like the idea of maintaining a pool of 6/7 bones (since 
> that's what Pasi says he has on his public server), regardless of how 
> many bones had before running Hearse.  To me, it seems to cover the goal 
> of mimicking a multi-user machine more elegantly.

I seem to recall reading a summary of a discussion regarding the number 
of bones to be expected on a multi-user machine vis-a-vis a single-user 
machine. (Thought it was on the myths and facts page, but neither that 
nor a Google search yields it.) Iirc, the upshot of the piece was that a 
multi-user system will have more bones created, but more bones will also 
be used up by other players as well. An individual player on such a 
system would thus likely encounter bones at about the same frequency as 
he would on his own system; the difference being that the bones he 
encounters would not tend usually to be his own. If there are several 
good players on that multi-user system, the individual player might even 
encounter *fewer* bones than on his own system, because players who 
ascend would use up bones without leaving any.

Therefore, I would suggest that the current behavior of Hearse (swapping 
files without adding many extra) *does* mimic a multi-user system--with 
the option of keeping your own bones files turned off, but with the 
possibility of downloading your own bones files restored.

